diff --git a/apps/reax/docs/decisions/0010-runtime-capture.md b/apps/reax/docs/decisions/0010-runtime-capture.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f75a8a4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/apps/reax/docs/decisions/0010-runtime-capture.md
@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# 0010. RUN: capturing and comparing a process reax itself starts
+
+Status: proposed (W2-5, RUN, first slice; not yet reviewed or run in CI). Proposed as D-37 (the
+slice, its names and rows, no attach mode), D-38 (the approval gate), D-39 (child-tree observation),
+D-40 (the answer key) and D-41 (refusal doctests and the `"run"` example workspace). The Task
+Master records them in `docs/implementation/DECISION-LOG.md` after review; numbers may change.
+(0009 is held by parked COD Part B.)
+
+## Context
+
+rea's REA-C22 captures "command + actions → frames, exit, filesystem before/after" through a PTY
+and a headless terminal. The user's hard limit: RUN inspects only processes that reax's own tests
+start; Frida (REA-C16) and ADB (REA-C26) stay BLOCKED_EXTERNAL. The sandbox (ADR 0006) already
+runs a child under an enforced policy and owns its tree kill on POSIX. Only Node 24 built-ins are
+used; `packages/core` is unchanged (its `ApprovalQueue` and `Kernel` are used as they are).
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Two operations.** `observe.process` (tool `observe`, effect `exec`, cost `deep`, untrusted)
+  runs one program from the workspace, optionally with this reax's Node, in a fresh scratch
+  directory (`mkdtemp(<tmpdir>/reax-run-)`, removed in `finally`) under `runSandboxed(...,
+  {required: true})`, and returns a `reax.capture/1` document. `compare.process` (tool `compare`,
+  effect `read`, cost `cheap`) reads two capture files (8 MiB each at most), validates them field
+  by field (unknown fields, an unknown `format` or a malformed value are `bad_request`), and
+  returns `{equal, differences[], timing: {a, b}}`.
+- **reax.capture/1:** `argv` (as approved: the program relative to the workspace, `node` as a
+  label), `enforcement`, `exit {code, signal, timedOut}`, `stdout {length, sha256, text, truncated}`
+  (bytes from core's `captureStdoutAsBytes`; `text` only when valid UTF-8 of 64 KiB or less),
+  `stderr {text, truncated}` (core keeps no bytes for stderr), `files[] {path, type, size, sha256,
+  target}`, `tree {processes[] {pid, pgid, parent, depth, name, reparented}, complete}`,
+  `coverage`, `limitations`, and `timing {durationMs, samples}`, kept apart. Partial: cut output,
+  a deadline, a stop, lossy stderr, a cut walk, a partial tree, or sampling past its cap.
+- **Equality** covers argv, enforcement, exit, stdout (sha256 and length), stderr, the file set
+  and the tree's shape (the multiset of (depth, name, parent's name), pids ignored). Timing,
+  coverage and limitations are never part of it (REA-C22-LIMIT).
+  compare.process does not normalise pids anywhere else: a file whose content holds pids (the
+  fixture's `tree/*.json`) differs between two genuine runs, so two genuine captures of the
+  scenario never compare equal. The committed equal pair is synthetic (a capture and a copy with
+  renumbered tree pids and other timing; `fixtures/run/README.md`).
+- **The gate (D-38).** In order, before anything is created or started: no `OpContext.approvals`
+  (internal, like `signal`) is `runtime_approval_required`; no enforced sandbox is
+  `sandbox_unavailable` with no approval asked (always so on win32); a program outside the
+  workspace is `bad_request`; then one `ApprovalQueue.request` with `detail {argv, cwd:
+  "<scratch>", timeoutMs, backend}`; a denial or expiry is core's `approval_denied` or
+  `approval_expired`. One approval per run; no auto rules, flag or environment variable. The CLI,
+  `reax serve` and the scheduler never set `approvals`, so RUN is off on every production surface
+  in this slice.
+- **Owned roots only (D-37).** `src/run/owned.ts` holds the live roots this process spawned: a pid
+  enters in the sandbox's new `onSpawn` hook and leaves in the root's exit turn. `ownedTree(pid)`
+  and `stopOwned(pid)` refuse any other pid with `process_not_owned` before running ps or sending
+  a signal. `stopOwned` aborts the run's own signal, so the sandbox kills the tree only while the
+  root is unreaped. There is no attach mode and no pid argument.
+- **The child tree (D-39).** The global snapshot is `ps -A -o pid=,ppid=,pgid=` only. Names are
+  asked for the root and its descendants only, with the field that holds the kernel's name for the
+  process, never its argv: on macOS `ps -o pid=,ppid=,pgid=,ucomm=,pid= -p ...` (ucomm is the accounting
+  name `p_comm`, the executed file's name, at most 16 bytes, padded by ps); macOS's `comm` is not
+  used because it prints argv[0] from the process's argument area, part of its command line
+  (verified: a process started with argv0 `x` shows comm `x`, ucomm its file name). On Linux
+  `ps -o pid=,ppid=,pgid=,comm=,pid= -p ...`: procps-ng's comm is the kernel task name
+  (`/proc/<pid>/stat` field 2, the executed file's name, at most 15 bytes, changeable by the
+  process with `prctl(PR_SET_NAME)`), not argv (procps-ng ps(1), proc(5); taken from the
+  documentation, not run on Linux here). Descendants are the root's group plus the ppid closure
+  from the root and its group members; after the root's reap, a process listed with its pid is
+  discarded with its ppid chain, so its children are never named. Snapshots are taken at spawn,
+  every 100 ms (600 at most, run synchronously), and in the root's exit turn through the new
+  `onRootExit` hook, before the group kill; that last one is what sees the fixture's child and
+  grandchild deterministically. Identity is (pid, pgid). The parsing rules (cut output, unreadable
+  or split lines, duplicate or unasked pids, disagreeing names lines, escaped names,
+  procps-ng's zombie form `name <defunct>`, the root missing, pid reuse, the 256-process cap) are in
+  `src/run/tree.ts`; a snapshot with any problem is taken again once, and a second problem makes
+  the tree partial. Stated scope: processes alive at a snapshot. Every names record is framed: the
+  query prints the pid again after the name, and a line counts only when its leading and trailing
+  pids agree; any other line voids every name from that query (the tree is partial). This matters
+  on macOS, whose ucomm prints a name's line break raw (16 bytes are enough for a name to print a
+  line that reads as the record of a requested pid that exited between the two ps calls);
+  procps-ng escapes control bytes as `?`. Whether procps-ng accepts the repeated `pid` column is
+  taken from its documentation (a format list may repeat a field) and confirmed only by CI's Linux
+  lane; if it did not, the names query fails and the tree is partial, never complete. macOS's ucomm
+  shows a zombie's real name; the `<defunct>` and `(comm)` checks (BSD comm forms, not used) stay as
+  defensive checks. A name with bytes that are not UTF-8 (U+FFFD after decoding) is null. Residual window: a pid reused
+  between the global snapshot and the names query by a process with the same ppid and pgid (a
+  sibling in the same group) gets the newcomer's name; it is still a descendant of the owned root.
+- **Sandbox hooks.** `SandboxRunOptions` gains `onSpawn(pid)`, `onRootExit(pid)` (called in the
+  exit turn before `TREE.killGroupAtExit`; a throwing hook does not skip the kill, and
+  runSandboxed rejects with its error once the run has ended) and `captureStdoutAsBytes`
+  (`SandboxResult.stdoutBytes`). Absent, nothing changes.
+- **The scratch walk** lists by lstat, follows no link, never opens a fifo, opens regular files
+  with `O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK` and checks them again with fstat; caps 10,000 entries, 64 levels,
+  64 MiB hashed.
+- **Answer key (D-40).** `bench/oracle/run.json` from `scripts/oracle-run.py` (Python stdlib, a
+  separate process, no ps): it runs `test/fixtures/run/scenario.mjs` in a new session and records
+  exit, output, files (tree records marked dynamic) and the tree's shape from the fixture's own
+  records, then kills the fixture's group by its exact id. CI re-derives it with
+  `scripts/oracle.ts --check`. At test time, the pids and parent links reax observed are checked
+  against the fixture's own `tree/*.json`.
+- **Refusal doctests (D-41).** `Example.expectError` documents a refusal and is checked as a
+  doctest; `Example.workspace` gains `"run"` (`fixtures/run`).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Rows `RX-RUN/gate`, `capture`, `tree`, `owned`, `cleanup` (linux, darwin, as `RX-SBX/enforced`)
+  and `compare` (all three). No rea or leanrea row is tagged: there is no PTY, no actions and no
+  frames, so `rea.tool/capture_process_scenario` and `rea.tool/compare_process_captures` are not
+  satisfied as written. `REA-C22-LIMIT` gets its `[+]` and `[-]` tests from `compare.process`.
+- Out of scope: a PTY, input actions and frames; stdin; network; Windows capture (SBX-W); an
+  approval channel in the daemon, CLI or MCP; attaching to any existing process; Frida, ADB, CDP,
+  Electron and native UI; memory limits; persisting captures.
+- Known limits: ps runs synchronously at each sample (fine in process; a daemon channel would want
+  an asynchronous sampler); a process that changes its process group is recorded as a second
+  entry (partial); a name with `\`, `?` or a control byte is reported null; a comm in parentheses
+  (BSD ps's form for an exiting process it cannot read; also Linux names such as `(sd-pam)`) gives no
+  name from that snapshot.
+
+**Reopen when** a daemon approval channel is added, a PTY slice arrives, or SBX-W lands.
