+# RUN example captures (`compare.process`)
+
+`compare.process`'s doctest and the RUN compare tests read these two `reax.capture/1` files (ADR
+0010).
+
+- `scenario-a.json` is a real capture made on macOS (sandbox-exec) by `observe.process` with
+  `{program: "scenario.mjs", interpreter: "node", args: ["alpha", "b c"]}` in the workspace
+  `test/fixtures/run`, approved through core's `ApprovalQueue` on an in-memory kernel, with
+  temporary `HOME`, `TMPDIR` and `REAX_HOME`.
+- `scenario-b.json` is a copy of it with every pid in `tree.processes` renumbered (50001, 50002,
+  50003; parents and pgids renumbered the same way) and a different `timing` (`durationMs` + 1234,
+  `samples` + 7). Nothing else differs, so the two compare equal: pids are not part of the tree's
+  shape, and timing is never compared. The pair is synthetic: two genuine captures of the scenario
+  never compare equal, because the files `tree/*.json` hold the run's pids, so their sha256 (and
+  possibly size) differ, and `compare.process` normalises pids only in the tree's shape, nowhere
+  else.
+
+The tests derive every unequal pair from `scenario-a.json` in memory (a changed exit, stdout, file,
+or tree). Its exit, stdout, stderr, files and tree shape are checked against the answer key
+`bench/oracle/run.json` (`test/run-compare.test.ts`). These files are not regenerated in CI.
